Extension API: append the owner of every ship icon to STATE_SNAPSHOT.

STATUS: OPTIONAL. Do not apply this unless you want it.

    OrionLayer does NOT need this patch. Corrected 29 August 2026
    after actually reading shipstak.cpp instead of inferring from the
    call chain.

WHY IT LOOKED NECESSARY
    s_ship_icon has no owner field. On the C++ side the galaxy map
    resolves it as

        _ship_icon[i].node_idx
          -> MOX::_ship_node[node].ship_idx
            -> MOX::_ship[ship].owner

    and MOX::_ship_node[] is not serialized.

WHY IT IS NOT NECESSARY
    _ship_node is a pure function of _ship[], which IS serialized.
    SHIPSTAK::Find_Ship_Stacks_ (shipstak.cpp:45) allocates nodes
    strictly sequentially:

        next_free = _next_free_node;
        _ship_node[next_free].ship_idx = i;
        _next_free_node++;

    in BOTH branches of the loop, so node N is just the N-th ship with
    status < 3, in array order. The location/x/y/owner comparison picks
    which stack a ship joins, never which node it gets. And nothing
    outside shipstak.cpp ever writes _ship_node[].ship_idx.

    screens/galaxy_map/ships.py rebuilds the map from that rule and
    validates it: Ship_Stack_Star_Id_ (shipstak.cpp:25) is literally
    _ship[...].location, and Build_Ship_Icons_ copies that into
    star_idx, so every icon carries its own checksum.

WHAT THE PATCH IS STILL GOOD FOR
    It removes the reconstruction and the one case the reconstruction
    cannot cover: a snapshot taken after _ship[] changed but before
    Find_Ship_Stacks_ ran again. The validation catches that and falls
    back to neutral colours; this patch would just be right. Costs one
    byte per icon against 45 KB for serializing _ship_node itself.

WHERE
    At the very END of ext::SerializeState(), after the ng_* block.
    That position is load-bearing: an older client stops reading after
    ng_antarans and is unaffected, and a newer client reading an
    unpatched build simply finds no bytes left. Inserting the block
    next to the ship icons instead would shift the ng_* offsets and
    break the New Game screen.

    Everything stays read-only; no game global is written.

--- a/src/ext/ext_api.cpp
+++ b/src/ext/ext_api.cpp
@@
     Write16(buf, NEWGAME::_random_events_button_variable);
     Write16(buf, NEWGAME::_xenon_attacks_button_variable);
+
+    // Ship icon owners: one uint8 per icon, 0xFF = unresolvable.
+    // s_ship_icon stores only node_idx; the owner sits two hops away
+    // through _ship_node, which is not serialized. Appended LAST so
+    // clients built against the older snapshot keep working.
+    for (int i = 0; i < MOX::_ship_icon_count; i++) {
+        int16_t node = MOX::_ship_icon[i].node_idx;
+        int16_t ship = (node >= 0 && node < MAX_SHIPS)
+                     ? MOX::_ship_node[node].ship_idx : -1;
+        Write8(buf, (ship >= 0 && ship < MAX_SHIPS)
+                    ? (uint8_t)MOX::_ship[ship].owner : 0xFF);
+    }
 }

VERIFY
    Snapshot length grows by exactly _ship_icon_count bytes. With the
    game on the galaxy map:

        python tools/ext_diag.py

    and in OrionLayer the fleets take the player colours instead of
    grey. No colour change means the block is absent — check that the
    build really picked up the patch, not that the client is wrong
    (lesson: "check that the file you copied is the file you built").
